# Line endings
#
# core.autocrlf is often true on Windows, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout.
# That breaks shell scripts: a CRLF shebang makes bash fail with
# "bad interpreter: ...^M", so `make msi` / `make nsis` would fail on any
# fresh clone made on Windows. Force LF for anything the shell executes.
*.sh text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
*.mk text eol=lf

# Go source must stay LF. gofmt compares bytes, so a CRLF checkout makes
# `gofmt -l` flag every file on a Windows clone even though the content is
# unchanged. That turns the format check into a machine-dependent failure.
*.go text eol=lf

# PowerShell is executed only by Windows, where CRLF is native.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Release artefacts. Marking them binary stops git from applying any
# line-ending conversion to files that must survive a round trip byte for byte.
*.deb binary
*.rpm binary
*.apk binary
*.msi binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.zip binary
